      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Complexity of Audio Data",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Complexity of Audio Data",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "KjZu6FL5OnrGEyuFIyYO9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -305.74241518252643,
      "y": 153.93273727871406,
      "width": 3345.490478515625,
      "height": 1050,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "SotktBDthKKFSOCnG_F2e",
        "7Zwi3_fQiswTLVP2WQtx1"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "Yzy",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2076986143,
      "version": 4274,
      "versionNonce": 944928100,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790174269684,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do you make tokens for audio? \n\nToken-based models expect a 1D sequence from a finite set of symbols, but which are the \nbest symbols?\n\nThe input to a audio tokenizer, like the input to the human ear, is a complex series of\nchanges in air pressure. We represent sound waves by plotting the change in air pressure \nover time.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe first step in digitizing a sound wave like this is to convert the analog representations. \nThis analog-to-digital conversion has two steps: sampling and quantization. \n\nIn sampling, we measure its amplitude at a particular time; the sampling rate is the number \nof samples taken per second.\n\n                                    1 kHz = 1,000 samples per second\n\nAmplitude measurements are usually stored as integers for efficiency, either 8 bit (values \nfrom -128–127) or 16 bit (values from -32768–32767). \n\nThis process of representing real-valued numbers as integers is called quantization because \nthe difference between two integers acts as a minimum granularity (a quantum size) and all \nvalues that are closer together than this quantum size are represented identically. \n\n\n    ❌ bits: 01000011011011110110110001101111011100100110110001100101011100110111001100100000011001110111001001100101011001010110111000100000011010010110010001100101011000010111001100100000011100110110110001100101011001010111000000100000011001100111010101110010011010010110111101110101011100110110110001111001\n    ❌ bytes: [67, 111, 108, 111, 114, 108, 101, 115, 115, 32, 103, 114, 101, 101, 110, 32, 105, 100, 101, 97, 115, 32, 115, 108, 101, 101, 112, 32, 102, 117, 114, 105, 111, 117, 115, 108, 121]\n\n\nNow we have a finite set of 2 symbols but very long senquences. For example 16 kHz speech \nstored in 16-bit format could be thought of as a series of 65,536 (2^16) symbols, with \n16,000 of those symbols per second of speech.\n\nA system that generates 16,000 symbols per second makes the speech signal too long to be \nfeasibly processed by  a attention-based models!",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do you make tokens for audio? \n\nToken-based models expect a 1D sequence from a finite set of symbols, but which are the \nbest symbols?\n\nThe input to a audio tokenizer, like the input to the human ear, is a complex series of\nchanges in air pressure. We represent sound waves by plotting the change in air pressure \nover time.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe first step in digitizing a sound wave like this is to convert the analog representations. \nThis analog-to-digital conversion has two steps: sampling and quantization. \n\nIn sampling, we measure its amplitude at a particular time; the sampling rate is the number \nof samples taken per second.\n\n                                    1 kHz = 1,000 samples per second\n\nAmplitude measurements are usually stored as integers for efficiency, either 8 bit (values \nfrom -128–127) or 16 bit (values from -32768–32767). \n\nThis process of representing real-valued numbers as integers is called quantization because \nthe difference between two integers acts as a minimum granularity (a quantum size) and all \nvalues that are closer together than this quantum size are represented identically. \n\n\n    ❌ bits: 01000011011011110110110001101111011100100110110001100101011100110111001100100000011001110111001001100101011001010110111000100000011010010110010001100101011000010111001100100000011100110110110001100101011001010111000000100000011001100111010101110010011010010110111101110101011100110110110001111001\n    ❌ bytes: [67, 111, 108, 111, 114, 108, 101, 115, 115, 32, 103, 114, 101, 101, 110, 32, 105, 100, 101, 97, 115, 32, 115, 108, 101, 101, 112, 32, 102, 117, 114, 105, 111, 117, 115, 108, 121]\n\n\nNow we have a finite set of 2 symbols but very long senquences. For example 16 kHz speech \nstored in 16-bit format could be thought of as a series of 65,536 (2^16) symbols, with \n16,000 of those symbols per second of speech.\n\nA system that generates 16,000 symbols per second makes the speech signal too long to be \nfeasibly processed by  a attention-based models!",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "UvdQudovaviMqtKgt9SeA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -349.71089849207124,
      "y": -527.8931207656864,
      "width": 1021.4190673828125,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "Yzz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1703194133,
      "version": 3474,
      "versionNonce": 620284636,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790170576056,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The way token-based systems address this task is identical to language modeling. As the\ntext counterpart, it is used an audio tokenizer to create discrete audio tokens from speech \nIn the literature these tokens are referred to as acoustic tokens.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThese tokens are then fed into the model (token encoding) and can be retrieved later (token decoding).\n\nHaving discrete tokens brings some perks: \n    1. we can make use of language model technology: transformers, pretraining, fine-tuning, etc.\n    2. Simpler architecture --> Feed tokens into a transformer.\n    3. Tokens can capture emotion + noise + music",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The way token-based systems address this task is identical to language modeling. As the\ntext counterpart, it is used an audio tokenizer to create discrete audio tokens from speech \nIn the literature these tokens are referred to as acoustic tokens.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThese tokens are then fed into the model (token encoding) and can be retrieved later (token decoding).\n\nHaving discrete tokens brings some perks: \n    1. we can make use of language model technology: transformers, pretraining, fine-tuning, etc.\n    2. Simpler architecture --> Feed tokens into a transformer.\n    3. Tokens can capture emotion + noise + music",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "-341THf0jGphUwOzPEqcG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1724.9124786168231,
      "y": -530.5908261904357,
      "width": 996.2188720703125,
      "height": 2225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "zms2KZmWCFFWA3jWVWTwb"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "Z0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2004803950,
      "version": 3745,
      "versionNonce": 788467044,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790173103358,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "During training, a clean speech clip is gradually turned into random noise. \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model learns the reverse process — how to predict and remove small amounts of noise so \nthat, starting from pure noise, it can reconstruct natural speech.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn inference we input pure noise and ask the NN what is the noise to be removed to get the \naudio of the given input text. This is repeated for many iteration to incrementally remove noise\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nStyleTTS 2 models speech styles through diffusion models to generate the most suitable \nstyle for the text without requiring reference speech. The core innovation is how StyleTTS 2 uses \ndiffusion not to generate full waveforms iteratively but rather to sample a fixed-length latent “style”\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA major drawback of these approach is the complexity of the architecture. StyleTTS 2 comprises \nfive modules:\n1.  acoustic text encoder: receives phoneme-level BERT and represents phonetic information needed\nto synthesize the sounds\n2. prosodic text encoder, receives phoneme-level BERT and provides richer linguistic information\nuseful for duration and prosody.\n\n                                            Text:\n                                            \"The weather is beautiful today.\"\n                                            \n                                            Phoneme sequence:\n                                            /ð ə w ɛ ð ər .../\n\n3. Style Diffusion, represents style as a latent vector. The paper uses “style” very broadly: it can\ncontain information about prosody, lexical stress, speaking rate, formant transitions and other\ncharacteristics beyond phonetic content. Instead of deterministically predicting one style, it uses a\ndiffusion model to sample a plausible style conditioned on the text. StyleTTS 2 actually separates\nits style representation into acoustic style and prosodic style. The prosodic style is used by\nduration/prosody predictors, while acoustic style influences waveform generation.\n\n                                            \"This is amazing!\"\n                                                    ↓\n                                             Style diffusion\n                                                    ↓\n                                             expressive style vector\n                                                    ↓\n                                             energetic / appropriate speech\n\n3. Duration predictor: determines how long each phoneme should last.\n4. Prosody predictor, predicts characteristics such as pitch and energy.\n5. Decoder combines the information coming from the previous components\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "During training, a clean speech clip is gradually turned into random noise. \n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model learns the reverse process — how to predict and remove small amounts of noise so \nthat, starting from pure noise, it can reconstruct natural speech.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn inference we input pure noise and ask the NN what is the noise to be removed to get the \naudio of the given input text. This is repeated for many iteration to incrementally remove noise\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nStyleTTS 2 models speech styles through diffusion models to generate the most suitable \nstyle for the text without requiring reference speech. The core innovation is how StyleTTS 2 uses \ndiffusion not to generate full waveforms iteratively but rather to sample a fixed-length latent “style”\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA major drawback of these approach is the complexity of the architecture. StyleTTS 2 comprises \nfive modules:\n1.  acoustic text encoder: receives phoneme-level BERT and represents phonetic information needed to synthesize the sounds\n2. prosodic text encoder, receives phoneme-level BERT and provides richer linguistic information useful for duration and prosody.\n\n                                            Text:\n                                            \"The weather is beautiful today.\"\n                                            \n                                            Phoneme sequence:\n                                            /ð ə w ɛ ð ər .../\n\n3. Style Diffusion, represents style as a latent vector. The paper uses “style” very broadly: it can contain information about prosody, lexical stress, speaking rate, formant transitions and other characteristics beyond phonetic content. Instead of deterministically predicting one style, it uses a diffusion model to sample a plausible style conditioned on the text. StyleTTS 2 actually separates its style representation into acoustic style and prosodic style. The prosodic style is used by duration/prosody predictors, while acoustic style influences waveform generation.\n\n                                            \"This is amazing!\"\n                                                    ↓\n                                             Style diffusion\n                                                    ↓\n                                             expressive style vector\n                                                    ↓\n                                             energetic / appropriate speech\n\n3. Duration predictor: determines how long each phoneme should last.\n4. Prosody predictor, predicts characteristics such as pitch and energy.\n5. Decoder combines the information coming from the previous components\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
