      "updated": 1790173609403,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Compositional Approach",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Compositional Approach",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.15
    },
    {
      "id": "5uQEJaCcW1m2rGhK-oJnd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4064.6501874923706,
      "y": 3489.060363292694,
      "width": 1066.538818359375,
      "height": 1250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 50,
      "groupIds": [
        "1KAPx9N5VS9-6jXwstOHY"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1xV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1238890325,
      "version": 3758,
      "versionNonce": 718788196,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790173609403,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Meta experimented with this compositional approach to integrate speech capabilities into Llama 3. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn the input side, the speech module consists of two successive modules: a speech encoder and an adapter.\n\nThe speech encoder is a Conformer model with 1B \nparameters. The input to the model consists of \n80-dimensional mel-spectrogram features.\n\nThe speech adapter contains about 100M parameters.\nIt is composed of a convolution layer,\na Transformer layer, and a linear layer to give even more\nfine-grained features to the language model.\n\nThe output of the speech module is directly fed into the \nlanguage model as token representation, enabling\ndirect interaction between speech and text tokens. \nThe speech module generates embeddings that can be \nseamlessly integrated with text tokens, enabling the \nspeech interface to leverage all the capabilities of \nthe Llama 3 language model.\n\nOn the output side, they use Llama 3 8B embeddings in two \nkey components for speech generation: Text Normalization and\nProsody Modeling.\n\nThe text normalization (TN) module carries out transformation from written-form text into the spoken form\nFor example, text written as 123 --> one hundred twenty three or one two three depending\non the context. The TN system consists of a streaming LSTM-based sequence-tagging model that\npredicts the sequence of handcrafted TN rules used to transform the input text.\n\nThe Prosody modeling is a a decoder-only Transformer-based Prosody model (PM) takes the Llama 3 \nembeddings for the prediction of three prosodic features: \n    - log duration of each phone, \n    - log F0 (fundamental frequency) average, \n    - and log power average across the phone duration.\nThe PM training data includes linguistic and prosodic features extracted from a 50K-hour TTS dataset\nbut the code was never released.\n\n\nQwen3 tts",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Meta experimented with this compositional approach to integrate speech capabilities into Llama 3. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOn the input side, the speech module consists of two successive modules: a speech encoder and an adapter.\n\nThe speech encoder is a Conformer model with 1B \nparameters. The input to the model consists of \n80-dimensional mel-spectrogram features.\n\nThe speech adapter contains about 100M parameters.\nIt is composed of a convolution layer,\na Transformer layer, and a linear layer to give even more\nfine-grained features to the language model.\n\nThe output of the speech module is directly fed into the \nlanguage model as token representation, enabling\ndirect interaction between speech and text tokens. \nThe speech module generates embeddings that can be \nseamlessly integrated with text tokens, enabling the \nspeech interface to leverage all the capabilities of \nthe Llama 3 language model.\n\nOn the output side, they use Llama 3 8B embeddings in two \nkey components for speech generation: Text Normalization and\nProsody Modeling.\n\nThe text normalization (TN) module carries out transformation from written-form text into the spoken form\nFor example, text written as 123 --> one hundred twenty three or one two three depending\non the context. The TN system consists of a streaming LSTM-based sequence-tagging model that\npredicts the sequence of handcrafted TN rules used to transform the input text.\n\nThe Prosody modeling is a a decoder-only Transformer-based Prosody model (PM) takes the Llama 3 \nembeddings for the prediction of three prosodic features: \n    - log duration of each phone, \n    - log F0 (fundamental frequency) average, \n    - and log power average across the phone duration.\nThe PM training data includes linguistic and prosodic features extracted from a 50K-hour TTS dataset\nbut the code was never released.\n\n\nQwen3 tts",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
