    {
      "id": "bjl5ggpCVK8RJR9ALRpI4",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -362.51697183790657,
      "y": 3585.9749642951147,
      "width": 970.9990234375,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "QKSWLfstFDS5VE8QAxc2f",
        "Ad5uIsbwE95S3zFoRVhkv"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2UK",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2100204713,
      "version": 4373,
      "versionNonce": 2135433572,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790170801957,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Problem: Due to the complexity of speech communication (linguistic + paralinguistic), the single \ncodebook given by VQ doesn’t produce good enough reconstructions in practice.\n\nSolution --> Residual Vector Quantization\nRun standard VQ with a codebook, then take the codebook vector and substract the original \nvector: this is the residual \n    --> error between the codebook and original vector; \n    --> the information of the original vector that the VQ didn’t capture. \n\nNow we then take that residual vector and pass it through another vector quantizer! That gives \nus a second codeword that represents the residual part of the vector. We then take the residual \nfrom the second codeword, and do this again, and again, and again... Until we get N codewords for \na single starting vector.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Problem: Due to the complexity of speech communication (linguistic + paralinguistic), the single \ncodebook given by VQ doesn’t produce good enough reconstructions in practice.\n\nSolution --> Residual Vector Quantization\nRun standard VQ with a codebook, then take the codebook vector and substract the original \nvector: this is the residual \n    --> error between the codebook and original vector; \n    --> the information of the original vector that the VQ didn’t capture. \n\nNow we then take that residual vector and pass it through another vector quantizer! That gives \nus a second codeword that represents the residual part of the vector. We then take the residual \nfrom the second codeword, and do this again, and again, and again... Until we get N codewords for \na single starting vector.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ddDYycH55rdDi4o2ha-Ul",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -290.7821226173005,
      "y": 3495.2277744213693,
      "width": 805.6166538900814,
      "height": 45.474285714285024,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
