    {
      "id": "n12mhMC85qOLuyaR78GGL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -348.3487240189611,
      "y": 1373.2328613939685,
      "width": 1003.8980102539062,
      "height": 1650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "sCkir82V55TiFktdcvyTY"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7x2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1409699625,
      "version": 4744,
      "versionNonce": 1501972196,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790170713426,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Acoustic tokens are created from a technique called vector quantization.\n\nWe are NOT talking about Scalar Quantization! 🙅🙅\nScalar Quantization a lossy data compression technique that converts high-precision floating-point\nnumbers (such as 32-bit floats) into smaller, discrete integer types (such as 8-bit integers).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe goal of Vector Quantization (VQ) is to assign each new vector to a predefined quantized vecoter\nbeing part of a finite set codebook.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the simplest VQ algorithm, we use the k-means algorithm to learn the quantized vectores.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGoal: dividie a large set of points (vectors) into groups having approximately the same number of\npoints closest to them. Each group is represented by its centroid point, like a spokesperson for that\ngroup.\n\n\nTRAINING\nThe k-means algorithm starts by assigning a random vector to each cluster k. Then there are two\niterative steps.\n\n1. Assignment step: given a set of k current centroids and the entire dataset of vectors, each vector\nis assigned to the cluster whose codeword is the closest estimation step.\n2. Clustering calculation: the codeword for each cluster is recomputed by recalculating a new mean\nvector. \n\nINFERENCE\nWhen a new vector comes in, we compare it to each vector in the codebook. Whichever codeword is\nclosest, we assign it to that codeword’s associated cluster.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Acoustic tokens are created from a technique called vector quantization.\n\nWe are NOT talking about Scalar Quantization! 🙅🙅\nScalar Quantization a lossy data compression technique that converts high-precision floating-point numbers (such as 32-bit floats) into smaller, discrete integer types (such as 8-bit integers).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe goal of Vector Quantization (VQ) is to assign each new vector to a predefined quantized vecoter being part of a finite set codebook.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the simplest VQ algorithm, we use the k-means algorithm to learn the quantized vectores.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGoal: dividie a large set of points (vectors) into groups having approximately the same number of points closest to them. Each group is represented by its centroid point, like a spokesperson for that group.\n\n\nTRAINING\nThe k-means algorithm starts by assigning a random vector to each cluster k. Then there are two iterative steps.\n\n1. Assignment step: given a set of k current centroids and the entire dataset of vectors, each vector is assigned to the cluster whose codeword is the closest estimation step.\n2. Clustering calculation: the codeword for each cluster is recomputed by recalculating a new mean vector. \n\nINFERENCE\nWhen a new vector comes in, we compare it to each vector in the codebook. Whichever codeword is closest, we assign it to that codeword’s associated cluster.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
