    {
      "id": "gDmdvLw4ua9fvBTETKZsF",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1922.3790350694526,
      "y": 1398.2275358260194,
      "width": 1003.8980102539062,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "ePrUS0zuj7_DK9u8JkKwL",
        "LtdCKoSRLglkpsgX5fllV"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8s",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1033577530,
      "version": 5315,
      "versionNonce": 1263412453,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790084287175,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We can do better than k-means...\n\nThe standard way to learn audio tokens is from a neural audio codec.\n\nAudio codec belong to the family of autoencoders which is a neural network that is trained to\nattempt to copy its input to its output. In doing so however it learns to encode data into a compact\nlatent representation that laer use to decode that representation to reconstruct the original data.\nBecause the model is forced to prioritize which aspects of the input should be copied, it learns useful\nproperties of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSoundStream is a good example of this category. Its has three stages:\n\n1. an encoder maps the acoustic waveform, a series of T values x = x1,x2,...,xT ,\nto a sequence of τ embeddings z = z1,z2,...,zτ .\n2. a vector quantizer that takes each embedding zt corresponding to part of the waveform, and\nrepresents it by a sequence of discrete tokens each taken from one of the Nc codebooks\n3. a decoder that generates a lossy reconstructed waveform span xˆ from the quantizer output\nvector zqt",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We can do better than k-means...\n\nThe standard way to learn audio tokens is from a neural audio codec.\n\nAudio codec belong to the family of autoencoders which is a neural network that is trained to attempt to copy its input to its output. In doing so however it learns to encode data into a compact latent representation that laer use to decode that representation to reconstruct the original data.\nBecause the model is forced to prioritize which aspects of the input should be copied, it learns useful properties of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSoundStream is a good example of this category. Its has three stages:\n\n1. an encoder maps the acoustic waveform, a series of T values x = x1,x2,...,xT ,\nto a sequence of τ embeddings z = z1,z2,...,zτ .\n2. a vector quantizer that takes each embedding zt corresponding to part of the waveform, and represents it by a sequence of discrete tokens each taken from one of the Nc codebooks\n3. a decoder that generates a lossy reconstructed waveform span xˆ from the quantizer output vector zqt",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
