    {
      "id": "qYF23pitKP-MisDEy7LIA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1135.5095699531967,
      "y": 2402.1750927174153,
      "width": 625.9041748046875,
      "height": 1825,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "bZaPekCxoZYlp3j0kKEYW",
        "KrpdU7Cs2TIrqnTdnRUsv",
        "fzk1lad6DCU1t9p8B5bsi",
        "yaMMPdDjK-06VO4q95okk",
        "DuPZTgoByo_XoCGH5Goie",
        "PlKZNLzdetuO09OyDuyRW",
        "LtdCKoSRLglkpsgX5fllV"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8w",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 403145387,
      "version": 3134,
      "versionNonce": 723251109,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790084287175,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Whisper operates on audio windows of approximately 30\nseconds.\n\nAll audio is re-sampled to 16,000 Hz, and an 80-channel log-\nmagnitude (128 in whisper v3) Mel spectrogram representation is\ncomputed on 25-millisecond windows with a stride of 10\nmilliseconds.\n\nA Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) uses a kernel, a small\nvector of weights w1 ...wk kernel , to extract features. Unlike a\n2D convolution, where the kernel moves across two spatial\ndimensions, a 1D convolution moves only along one dimension,\ntypically the temporal dimension.\n\nThe convolution of a kernel with a signal has 3 steps:\n\n1. Pass the kernel frame by frame (temporally) across the input\n2. At each frame computing the dot product of the kernel with\nthe local input values\n3. The output is the resulting sequence of dot products\n\nFor example, if the convolutional layer has a kernel width of 3,\nthe kernel processes three consecutive frames at each position.\n\n\nFrame 1     Frame 2     Frame 3     Frame 4     Frame 5\n   │                      \n   └────── Kernel ──────┘\n\n               ↓\n\n        one output value\n\nA convolution layer can also have a stride. The kernel then\nmoves forward along the sequence:\n\nFrame 1     Frame 2     Frame 3     Frame 4     Frame 5\n               │           │           │\n               └──────── Kernel ────────┘\n\n                           ↓\n\n                    next output value\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe encoder starts with a 1D convolution, followed by a\nsequence of strided 1D convolution for downsampling. The\nstrided convolutions therefore downsample by:\n\n                        2 × 4 × 5 × 8 = 320\n\nSo, with 24 kHz audio: 24000 / 320 = 75\n\nand the encoder produces 75 embeddings per second.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Whisper operates on audio windows of approximately 30 seconds.\n\nAll audio is re-sampled to 16,000 Hz, and an 80-channel log-\nmagnitude (128 in whisper v3) Mel spectrogram representation is computed on 25-millisecond windows with a stride of 10 milliseconds.\n\nA Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) uses a kernel, a small vector of weights w1 ...wk kernel , to extract features. Unlike a 2D convolution, where the kernel moves across two spatial dimensions, a 1D convolution moves only along one dimension, typically the temporal dimension.\n\nThe convolution of a kernel with a signal has 3 steps:\n\n1. Pass the kernel frame by frame (temporally) across the input\n2. At each frame computing the dot product of the kernel with the local input values\n3. The output is the resulting sequence of dot products\n\nFor example, if the convolutional layer has a kernel width of 3, the kernel processes three consecutive frames at each position.\n\n\nFrame 1     Frame 2     Frame 3     Frame 4     Frame 5\n   │                      \n   └────── Kernel ──────┘\n\n               ↓\n\n        one output value\n\nA convolution layer can also have a stride. The kernel then moves forward along the sequence:\n\nFrame 1     Frame 2     Frame 3     Frame 4     Frame 5\n               │           │           │\n               └──────── Kernel ────────┘\n\n                           ↓\n\n                    next output value\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe encoder starts with a 1D convolution, followed by a sequence of strided 1D convolution for downsampling. The strided convolutions therefore downsample by:\n\n                        2 × 4 × 5 × 8 = 320\n\nSo, with 24 kHz audio: 24000 / 320 = 75\n\nand the encoder produces 75 embeddings per second.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
