      "text": "Cascade approach is bad for real-time interaction.\n\nThe core intuition: send speech and text in input as they develop.\n\nProblem\nSpeech and text as two timelines. Normally they do not line up cleanly:\n- audio is naturally chopped into uniform time slices, so audio is easy to index: every step is\n    just the next equal chunk of time.\n- text is not emitted at regular time intervals, it has irregular word/token timing. Some words\n    are short, some are long, some have pauses before them, and subword tokens can stretch\n    unevenly too. Text tokens represent linguistic units pronounced with varying durations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhy this causes trouble?\n\nImagine you want a streaming model to work step by step. \n\n                    time →      t1      t2      t3      t4      t5      t6\n                    \n                    audio →     A1      A2      A3      A4      A5      A6\n                    model →    step1   step2   step3   step4   step5   step6\n                    \n\nAt audio frame 4, what should the text side contain?\nThat is not obvious, because:\n- maybe the speaker is still finishing the previous word\n- maybe a new word starts here\n- maybe there is just silence\n- maybe one text token corresponds to several frames of audio",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Cascade approach is bad for real-time interaction.\n\nThe core intuition: send speech and text in input as they develop.\n\nProblem\nSpeech and text as two timelines. Normally they do not line up cleanly:\n- audio is naturally chopped into uniform time slices, so audio is easy to index: every step is\n    just the next equal chunk of time.\n- text is not emitted at regular time intervals, it has irregular word/token timing. Some words\n    are short, some are long, some have pauses before them, and subword tokens can stretch\n    unevenly too. Text tokens represent linguistic units pronounced with varying durations.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWhy this causes trouble?\n\nImagine you want a streaming model to work step by step. \n\n                    time →      t1      t2      t3      t4      t5      t6\n                    \n                    audio →     A1      A2      A3      A4      A5      A6\n                    model →    step1   step2   step3   step4   step5   step6\n                    \n\nAt audio frame 4, what should the text side contain?\nThat is not obvious, because:\n- maybe the speaker is still finishing the previous word\n- maybe a new word starts here\n- maybe there is just silence\n- maybe one text token corresponds to several frames of audio",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790087428644,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
