      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "z3Of4QKDs3mvqVQBLl4ds"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 248393052,
      "version": 1565,
      "versionNonce": 1085002972,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790169211893,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Contrary to standard TTS models, DSM wants a time-aligned text\nstream, something like:\n\n[PAD] [PAD] [WORD] [Hello] [PAD] [WORD] [world]\n\nProblem: who decides when to place the next WORD token? \n\nThe authors added an extra stream to the TTS outputs, whose\ngoal is to predict whether the next input text token will be a\n[WORD] token or not.\n\nSuppose the current word is “I want …”\nThe pause after “want” depends a lot on what comes next:\n\n   - “I want coffee” → probably no long pause\n   - “I want… to thank everyone” → maybe a different rhythm\n   -“I want, however, to note…” → pause changes again\n\nSo the model is deciding \"should I ask for the next word now, or\nwait a bit more?\"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe authors allows for speaker conditioning. A 10s  speaker audio\nextract is encoded with a speaker encoder and results in a speaker\nembedding with a fixed dimension. This is fed through cross-\nattention layers to the backbone.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Contrary to standard TTS models, DSM wants a time-aligned text stream, something like:\n\n[PAD] [PAD] [WORD] [Hello] [PAD] [WORD] [world]\n\nProblem: who decides when to place the next WORD token? \n\nThe authors added an extra stream to the TTS outputs, whose goal is to predict whether the next input text token will be a [WORD] token or not.\n\nSuppose the current word is “I want …”\nThe pause after “want” depends a lot on what comes next:\n\n   - “I want coffee” → probably no long pause\n   - “I want… to thank everyone” → maybe a different rhythm\n   -“I want, however, to note…” → pause changes again\n\nSo the model is deciding \"should I ask for the next word now, or wait a bit more?\"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe authors allows for speaker conditioning. A 10s  speaker audio extract is encoded with a speaker encoder and results in a speaker embedding with a fixed dimension. This is fed through cross-attention layers to the backbone.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790085095078,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
