    {
      "id": "sLGJBcixqhtGb1Blp7WZX",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8007.1211158315455,
      "y": 2312.4609860848977,
      "width": 717.7193874782987,
      "height": 2100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "UXONg1J5ODWwQj1xOjo5K"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bDr",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1497629404,
      "version": 1208,
      "versionNonce": 718675804,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790169237766,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Aaudio and text tokens are embedded separately and then their\nembeddings are summed at each time step.\nAt each 80 ms step, the decoder input is: audio embedding (current\nstep) + embedding of last generated token. This ties what you heard\nwith what you already said\n\nAt every step, the decoder produces exactly one token:\nEither:\n   [P] → “wait, not enough info yet”\n   [W] → “word boundary, start emitting text”\n   text tokens (subwords)\n\nThe model is conditioned on a target delay τ. It cannot emit text\nbefore τ has passed since the audio evidence\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn short-form transcription DSM-ASR is remarkably the only streaming\nmodel among top ASR systems from the OpenASR leaderboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn long form DSM-ASR outperforms streaming and non-streaming\nbaselines except for Parakeet, which it closely matches (7.8 vs 7.9).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe figure on the left compares the WER obtained for DSM-ASR with\nand without delay conditioning, along with Whisper-Streaming. We observe\nthat the delay of Whisper-Streaming has a large variance, while DSM-ASR\nhas a precision of ∼300ms around its target delay. \n\nThe figure on the right shows the throughput on an H100 GPU: DSM-\nASR can process 400 sequences simultaneously and its throughput is\nindependent of the delay.\nThis is unlike Whisper-Streaming which reduces its delay by re-evaluating\nthe partial input sequence more frequently, increasing the computational\ncost. Combined with the fact that Whisper-Streaming does not allow for\nbatching, this results in a 100x lower throughput than that of DSM-\nASR.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Aaudio and text tokens are embedded separately and then their embeddings are summed at each time step.\nAt each 80 ms step, the decoder input is: audio embedding (current step) + embedding of last generated token. This ties what you heard with what you already said\n\nAt every step, the decoder produces exactly one token:\nEither:\n   [P] → “wait, not enough info yet”\n   [W] → “word boundary, start emitting text”\n   text tokens (subwords)\n\nThe model is conditioned on a target delay τ. It cannot emit text before τ has passed since the audio evidence\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn short-form transcription DSM-ASR is remarkably the only streaming model among top ASR systems from the OpenASR leaderboard.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn long form DSM-ASR outperforms streaming and non-streaming baselines except for Parakeet, which it closely matches (7.8 vs 7.9).\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe figure on the left compares the WER obtained for DSM-ASR with and without delay conditioning, along with Whisper-Streaming. We observe that the delay of Whisper-Streaming has a large variance, while DSM-ASR has a precision of ∼300ms around its target delay. \n\nThe figure on the right shows the throughput on an H100 GPU: DSM-ASR can process 400 sequences simultaneously and its throughput is independent of the delay.\nThis is unlike Whisper-Streaming which reduces its delay by re-evaluating the partial input sequence more frequently, increasing the computational cost. Combined with the fact that Whisper-Streaming does not allow for batching, this results in a 100x lower throughput than that of DSM-ASR.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790085095078,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
