      "locked": false,
      "text": "DELAYED  STREAMS MODELING",
      "fontSize": 109.47826086956503,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "DELAYED  STREAMS MODELING",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "2i5OmnaWnlUzqXZhfGpD2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1920.629517732229,
      "y": 2610.392352920603,
      "width": 918.5548502604171,
      "height": 2350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "7KtuFQXe23cN9lpdEIMpq"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bEtG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 140160122,
      "version": 5672,
      "versionNonce": 245628636,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790178736613,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Speech contains information at very different levels!\n\n    - verbal: what is being said\n    - paraverbal: how it saounds\n\n\nAudioLM makes this explicit by separating semantic tokens and acustic tokens\nAudioLM leverages two kinds of tokens. \n    1. coarse semantic tokens: speech units that correlate strongly with linguistic content\n    2. fine acoustic tokens try to preserve enough information to reconstruct the actual\n        sound.\n\nThe idea is that we are decoupling the task of the model which would otherwise have to to\ndiscover long-range linguistic structure while simultaneously worrying about tiny waveform\ndetails.\n\nTokenization\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAudioLM tokenizer extracts a much slower sequence of semantic tokens using representations\nfrom w2v-BERT XL (0.6B params). These are produced at only 25 Hz (25 tokens/sec),\nheavily downsampling the audio signal to allow for modeling long sequences. They used the\n7th layer in the MLM module of w2v-BERT XL and K = 1024 clusters as codes.\n\nAcoustic tokens are created with SoundStream which operates at 50Hz (50 token/sec) for\ninput waveforms at 16 kHz and uses multiple residual vector-quantization codebooks which\nconsists of a hierarchy of Q = 12 vector quantizers, each using a vocabulary of N = 1024\nsymbols.\n\nFurthermore acoustic tokens are divided in:\n    - Coarse acoustic tokens: the authors say that the earlier quantizers carry the big,\n        important acoustic information, while later quantizers progressively correct smaller\n        reconstruction errors.\n    - Fine acoustic tokens: more fine grained acustic features\n\n\n                                      one SoundStream frame at time t\n                                                      │\n                          ┌───────────────┴────────────────┐\n                          │                                                         │\n                      COARSE                                                 FINE\n                   RVQ 1 2 3 4                                        RVQ 5 6 7 ... 12\n                   ──────────                                ──────────────────\n                   main signal                                             residual detail\n        \n\nInference\n\nAudioLM models an audio sequence hierarchically, from semantic tokens up to fine acoustic\ntokens, by chaining several Transformer models, one for each stage. Each stage is trained for\nthe next token prediction based on past tokens.\n\nThe first stage performs this task on semantic tokens to model the high-level structure of\nthe audio sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the second stage, they concatenate the entire semantic token sequence, along with the\npast coarse acoustic tokens, and feed both as conditioning to the coarse acoustic model,\nwhich then predicts the future tokens. This step models acoustic properties such as speaker\ncharacteristics in speech or timbre in music.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the third stage, we process the coarse acoustic tokens with the fine acoustic model,\nwhich adds even more detail to the final audio. \nThe authors deliberately do not pass the semantic tokens into stage 3. Their assumption is\nthat once the coarse acoustic tokens are known, the fine acoustic details are sufficiently\ndetermined locally by those coarse tokens.\nFinally, we feed acoustic tokens to the SoundStream decoder to reconstruct a waveform.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Speech contains information at very different levels!\n\n    - verbal: what is being said\n    - paraverbal: how it saounds\n\n\nAudioLM makes this explicit by separating semantic tokens and acustic tokens\nAudioLM leverages two kinds of tokens. \n    1. coarse semantic tokens: speech units that correlate strongly with linguistic content\n    2. fine acoustic tokens try to preserve enough information to reconstruct the actual\n        sound.\n\nThe idea is that we are decoupling the task of the model which would otherwise have to to discover long-range linguistic structure while simultaneously worrying about tiny waveform details.\n\nTokenization\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAudioLM tokenizer extracts a much slower sequence of semantic tokens using representations from w2v-BERT XL (0.6B params). These are produced at only 25 Hz (25 tokens/sec), heavily downsampling the audio signal to allow for modeling long sequences. They used the 7th layer in the MLM module of w2v-BERT XL and K = 1024 clusters as codes.\n\nAcoustic tokens are created with SoundStream which operates at 50Hz (50 token/sec) for input waveforms at 16 kHz and uses multiple residual vector-quantization codebooks which consists of a hierarchy of Q = 12 vector quantizers, each using a vocabulary of N = 1024 symbols.\n\nFurthermore acoustic tokens are divided in:\n    - Coarse acoustic tokens: the authors say that the earlier quantizers carry the big,\n        important acoustic information, while later quantizers progressively correct smaller\n        reconstruction errors.\n    - Fine acoustic tokens: more fine grained acustic features\n\n\n                                      one SoundStream frame at time t\n                                                      │\n                          ┌───────────────┴────────────────┐\n                          │                                                         │\n                      COARSE                                                 FINE\n                   RVQ 1 2 3 4                                        RVQ 5 6 7 ... 12\n                   ──────────                                ──────────────────\n                   main signal                                             residual detail\n        \n\nInference\n\nAudioLM models an audio sequence hierarchically, from semantic tokens up to fine acoustic tokens, by chaining several Transformer models, one for each stage. Each stage is trained for the next token prediction based on past tokens.\n\nThe first stage performs this task on semantic tokens to model the high-level structure of the audio sequence.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the second stage, they concatenate the entire semantic token sequence, along with the past coarse acoustic tokens, and feed both as conditioning to the coarse acoustic model, which then predicts the future tokens. This step models acoustic properties such as speaker characteristics in speech or timbre in music.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn the third stage, we process the coarse acoustic tokens with the fine acoustic model, which adds even more detail to the final audio. \nThe authors deliberately do not pass the semantic tokens into stage 3. Their assumption is that once the coarse acoustic tokens are known, the fine acoustic details are sufficiently determined locally by those coarse tokens.\nFinally, we feed acoustic tokens to the SoundStream decoder to reconstruct a waveform.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
